1
00:00:06,539 --> 00:00:09,409
[engine revs]

2
00:00:11,378 --> 00:00:14,948
[Jerry] This is a 1967 Austin-Healey 3000
in ice blue.

3
00:00:17,050 --> 00:00:19,819
It has a straight-six,
150-horsepower engine.

4
00:00:21,588 --> 00:00:25,191
This is a car if you wanna say,
"I really love British sports cars.

5
00:00:25,258 --> 00:00:27,894
"And damn it, I will go down
with that ship if I have to."

6
00:00:31,833 --> 00:00:33,033
-Hello?
-Ricky?

7
00:00:33,199 --> 00:00:34,266
-Who's that?
-Jerry.

8
00:00:34,333 --> 00:00:35,333
[Ricky] Hello, man.

9
00:00:35,402 --> 00:00:36,536
-How's it going?
-[Jerry] What are you doing?

10
00:00:36,970 --> 00:00:40,106
[Ricky] Oh. Sitting out, I think I've got
irritable bowel syndrome.

11
00:00:40,199 --> 00:00:41,766
[Jerry] Oh, God, that is irritating.

12
00:00:41,808 --> 00:00:44,077
[Ricky laughs] Yeah.

13
00:00:44,177 --> 00:00:45,945
-[Jerry] You wanna grab a coffee?
-Yeah.

14
00:00:49,783 --> 00:00:53,920
I'm Jerry Seinfeld, and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

15
00:00:54,366 --> 00:00:56,733
Today my guest is my good friend
Ricky Gervais.

16
00:00:56,966 --> 00:00:59,766
He's a very funny, silly,
English comedian guy.

17
00:00:59,833 --> 00:01:00,800
[laughs]

18
00:01:00,933 --> 00:01:02,866
[Jerry]
And he's just funny. He makes me laugh.

19
00:01:02,929 --> 00:01:04,397
-What do you think?
-[Ricky] It's great.

20
00:01:05,131 --> 00:01:06,900
-English.
-Is it?

21
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:09,866
-Is it?
-I don't know anything.

22
00:01:09,903 --> 00:01:11,237
-You don't know anything about cars?
-No.

23
00:01:12,872 --> 00:01:14,507
-Is this the seat belt?
-What do you think so far?

24
00:01:14,574 --> 00:01:16,843
-I think it's a death trap.
-It is.

25
00:01:17,766 --> 00:01:20,300
There's no way there's a little air bag
in there, is there?

26
00:01:20,680 --> 00:01:23,817
-There is, but you have to blow it up.
-[laughing]

27
00:01:23,899 --> 00:01:25,800
Quick, we're gonna crash.

28
00:01:26,486 --> 00:01:27,587
Right.

29
00:01:30,924 --> 00:01:33,059
Well, I'm a very nervous passenger,
so let's...

30
00:01:34,166 --> 00:01:36,333
-Did you eat?
-I had some cereal.

31
00:01:36,663 --> 00:01:38,965
-That's so sweet.
-What?

32
00:01:39,032 --> 00:01:40,400
What kind of cereal?

33
00:01:41,866 --> 00:01:44,133
We can't be running out
of stuff to talk about already.

34
00:01:44,370 --> 00:01:46,706
If we're going into detail on the cereal,

35
00:01:47,566 --> 00:01:48,633
we're struggling.

36
00:01:51,177 --> 00:01:52,812
There's a dancing traffic cop.

37
00:01:52,899 --> 00:01:55,166
He's dancing, he's really
having a good time.

38
00:01:55,482 --> 00:01:56,950
She's dancing, too.

39
00:01:58,184 --> 00:01:59,619
We're in the middle of Fame.

40
00:02:01,066 --> 00:02:02,100
Irene Cara.

41
00:02:02,166 --> 00:02:03,966
Irene Cara.
She did the song, didn't she?

42
00:02:04,466 --> 00:02:05,433
Yes, Fame.

43
00:02:05,699 --> 00:02:07,233
I'm gonna live forever.

44
00:02:07,494 --> 00:02:09,996
-I'm gonna learn how to fly.
-Yeah.

45
00:02:10,063 --> 00:02:12,932
People are gonna see me and die.
Well, who's gonna pay for that?

46
00:02:13,366 --> 00:02:15,000
I think they meant it in a sort of...

47
00:02:15,466 --> 00:02:18,833
-I saw Irene Cara, I died.
-Ah, I see.

48
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:20,799
-Yeah, exactly.
-In a more like this way.

49
00:02:20,866 --> 00:02:22,533
-I died.
-I died.

50
00:02:22,575 --> 00:02:23,476
-Yeah.
-I see.

51
00:02:26,299 --> 00:02:27,500
Look at this guy.

52
00:02:27,614 --> 00:02:30,984
Nobody can explain
why the homeless have such fabulous hair.

53
00:02:31,050 --> 00:02:33,353
I love how you start
with "no one can explain."

54
00:02:33,433 --> 00:02:37,200
Like scientists have been working
around the clock for the last 25 years.

55
00:02:37,366 --> 00:02:39,266
They've spent billions
of government dollars,

56
00:02:39,325 --> 00:02:40,860
but they've not come up with anything.

57
00:02:40,927 --> 00:02:43,463
Like, who commissioned
this scientific study?

58
00:02:45,433 --> 00:02:47,033
Okay, here we go.

59
00:02:47,133 --> 00:02:48,201
[engine revving]

60
00:02:48,566 --> 00:02:50,166
No, be sensible.

61
00:02:51,804 --> 00:02:52,805
[Jerry] Oh, Jerry.

62
00:02:54,140 --> 00:02:55,041
Jesus.

63
00:02:55,108 --> 00:02:56,509
Sorry,

64
00:02:56,576 --> 00:02:58,011
are we in The Italian Job?

65
00:02:58,077 --> 00:02:59,279
Why are we doing this?

66
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:02,266
Oh, no, that made it worse. You--

67
00:03:02,715 --> 00:03:05,251
Jerry! For [bleep] sake!

68
00:03:05,685 --> 00:03:07,954
We're going 70 miles an hour.

69
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:10,200
We're not going 70.

70
00:03:11,566 --> 00:03:12,700
[Ricky] Let's take the bus.

71
00:03:14,233 --> 00:03:15,133
[Jerry] Oh, Jerry.

72
00:03:16,262 --> 00:03:17,163
Oh, God.

73
00:03:18,333 --> 00:03:22,366
Jerry, why is it so funny
to frighten a grown man?

74
00:03:23,333 --> 00:03:24,799
Fear is funny.

75
00:03:25,238 --> 00:03:28,141
-Fear is funny, yeah.
-Especially when it's not fake.

76
00:03:28,208 --> 00:03:31,477
We're so used to fake fear.
Everyone always acts scared,

77
00:03:31,544 --> 00:03:33,580
-pretends their scared.
-This is real, don't worry about it.

78
00:03:33,646 --> 00:03:36,182
I'm in a tin bucket with no suspension.

79
00:03:37,016 --> 00:03:38,184
Oh, Jesus.

80
00:03:38,685 --> 00:03:40,186
When was the last time
you were this scared?

81
00:03:40,253 --> 00:03:43,222
Doing award shows
and potentially ending my career is my...

82
00:03:43,323 --> 00:03:45,425
That's my adrenaline rush.

83
00:03:45,491 --> 00:03:47,660
I work for the Golden Globe committee.

84
00:03:48,928 --> 00:03:52,632
And this entire thing is a ruse
to get you to do the show again next year.

85
00:03:52,733 --> 00:03:53,733
Yeah.

86
00:03:53,799 --> 00:03:57,533
And I'm just gonna keep driving you
in this terrifying car until you agree.

87
00:03:58,566 --> 00:03:59,799
Oh, God.

88
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:03,466
I think the first couple,
they were sort of...

89
00:04:03,700 --> 00:04:05,566
They were taken aback.

90
00:04:06,233 --> 00:04:10,133
"Why is this Brit saying
all these awful things to us?"

91
00:04:10,466 --> 00:04:14,866
And then by the third one,
they go, "He's telling jokes. I get it."

92
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:17,599
What was the introduction for Mel Gibson?

93
00:04:18,266 --> 00:04:20,533
"I like a drink as much as the next man.

94
00:04:20,599 --> 00:04:22,466
"Unless the next man is Mel Gibson."

95
00:04:23,166 --> 00:04:24,500
That's a great joke.

96
00:04:24,566 --> 00:04:26,833
I like a joke that
sneaks up on you like that.

97
00:04:26,899 --> 00:04:31,537
Yeah. Oh, Jesus Christ! Ow!

98
00:04:31,604 --> 00:04:34,173
I can't understand
why this company went out of business

99
00:04:34,266 --> 00:04:36,466
shortly after this car was made.

100
00:04:37,566 --> 00:04:39,633
You really are holding on seriously there.

101
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:40,533
[Ricky] Yeah.

102
00:04:40,599 --> 00:04:43,666
It feels like I'm being dragged along
in a tin bath.

103
00:04:43,766 --> 00:04:45,500
That's what this feels like.

104
00:04:48,688 --> 00:04:52,191
Oh, God, you have to turn around
like an owl to see.

105
00:04:52,292 --> 00:04:54,961
There's no mirror in this car.

106
00:04:55,028 --> 00:04:56,429
Jerry, you can't...

107
00:04:56,496 --> 00:04:59,365
There's a mirror here.
I see exactly what I'm doing.

108
00:05:00,066 --> 00:05:02,233
-Oh, Christ.
-[Jerry] What are you worried about?

109
00:05:02,301 --> 00:05:03,236
[Ricky] What am I worried about?

110
00:05:05,271 --> 00:05:08,374
I was thinking maybe for the drive back,
we'd put the top down.

111
00:05:09,008 --> 00:05:10,009
Whatever.

112
00:05:10,910 --> 00:05:12,478
For [bleep] sake.

113
00:05:12,545 --> 00:05:14,280
Oh, come on, Jerry, this is mental.

114
00:05:14,380 --> 00:05:17,483
You don't need to do this.

115
00:05:17,984 --> 00:05:20,019
We're going for coffee, for Christ's sake.

116
00:05:20,166 --> 00:05:22,900
We're not going to diffuse a bomb, right?

117
00:05:23,099 --> 00:05:24,666
There's no need for this.

118
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:27,599
Even Jack Bauer had an hour, right?

119
00:05:30,029 --> 00:05:31,564
It's turned into a race now.

120
00:05:31,631 --> 00:05:35,168
-It's like a scene from Ronin.
-[laughing]

121
00:05:35,266 --> 00:05:37,766
That was a good laugh to start the day.

122
00:05:38,233 --> 00:05:40,900
My pain and suffering is
for everyone else.

123
00:05:41,300 --> 00:05:43,233
-I fall over for your amusement.
-Yes. Yes. Yes.

124
00:05:43,366 --> 00:05:45,133
-Of course.
-Now you got it.

125
00:05:45,266 --> 00:05:47,633
If someone's laughing with me or at me,
I've done my job.

126
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:48,681
-That's right.
-Yeah.

127
00:05:51,766 --> 00:05:55,733
[Jerry] The funniest thing
about the nervous passenger is

128
00:05:56,022 --> 00:05:59,325
that you think that that hand
could possibly do something.

129
00:05:59,425 --> 00:06:00,293
I know, yeah.

130
00:06:01,066 --> 00:06:03,200
Jesus Christ! And I'm an atheist.

131
00:06:07,834 --> 00:06:14,307
If you weren't a part of, arguably,
the most successful sitcom of all time...

132
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:16,599
Why is it arguable? What's the argument?

133
00:06:17,176 --> 00:06:19,312
And who's arguing on the other side,
I wanna know.

134
00:06:20,866 --> 00:06:23,766
If you weren't as successful as you are...

135
00:06:23,833 --> 00:06:24,933
Yeah?

136
00:06:25,299 --> 00:06:28,766
Rich, famous, loved, okay?

137
00:06:29,366 --> 00:06:30,866
Please, go on.

138
00:06:31,591 --> 00:06:34,127
You'd seem a bit like a maniac now.

139
00:06:34,193 --> 00:06:38,231
This is what mad people do,
drive and giggle at people's fear.

140
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:41,099
"Jerry Seinfeld wants you to do his show.
It's called...

141
00:06:41,233 --> 00:06:44,066
Couple of Comedians Going
for a Drive for Coffee. "Oh, great."

142
00:06:44,333 --> 00:06:47,566
"A man wants to drive you around New York
really fast

143
00:06:47,874 --> 00:06:50,143
in a death machine, laughing."

144
00:06:56,048 --> 00:06:57,984
Look at this, Ricky.
"Welcome to City Island."

145
00:06:58,533 --> 00:07:01,066
-Look at the nautical environment.
-Yeah. Lovely.

146
00:07:01,133 --> 00:07:03,066
-This is great. Come on.
-Yeah.

147
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:04,366
We've never been here.

148
00:07:04,490 --> 00:07:07,059
You would never ever come here
if it wasn't for me.

149
00:07:07,126 --> 00:07:08,127
No.

150
00:07:08,194 --> 00:07:11,330
I'm giving you a gift.
And I'm going to let you live, as well.

151
00:07:11,397 --> 00:07:14,066
-You've given me the gift of life.
-The gift of life.

152
00:07:16,369 --> 00:07:18,538
Look at this.

153
00:07:18,971 --> 00:07:21,207
I can see sort of an '80s film starting
like this...

154
00:07:21,274 --> 00:07:24,210
Where a writer comes
and then he gets embroiled.

155
00:07:25,344 --> 00:07:27,413
Oh, I love when someone gets embroiled.

156
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:29,615
I love people getting embroiled.

157
00:07:29,733 --> 00:07:31,200
They shouldn't have been embroiled.

158
00:07:31,299 --> 00:07:33,633
I'm going to a fishing village
and the first thing that happens is...

159
00:07:33,933 --> 00:07:34,766
They get embroiled.

160
00:07:34,821 --> 00:07:35,755
They get embroiled.

161
00:07:35,822 --> 00:07:39,926
And the reason people get embroiled is,
you never see the embroiling coming.

162
00:07:39,992 --> 00:07:40,993
No.

163
00:07:41,060 --> 00:07:43,296
You never can you see that
I'm about to get embroiled.

164
00:07:43,362 --> 00:07:44,397
[laughing]

165
00:07:48,467 --> 00:07:50,837
If you were in the electric chair,
they would ask you,

166
00:07:50,903 --> 00:07:52,271
"Do you have any last words?"

167
00:07:53,339 --> 00:07:54,841
How could you beat Gary Gilmore?

168
00:07:55,274 --> 00:07:58,044
His last words were, "Let's do it."

169
00:07:58,144 --> 00:07:59,145
-Really?
-Yeah.

170
00:08:00,513 --> 00:08:03,716
I really think the death penalty's
too depressing to even think about.

171
00:08:03,783 --> 00:08:05,117
I don't...

172
00:08:05,766 --> 00:08:08,066
I mean, I don't agree with it.

173
00:08:08,166 --> 00:08:11,799
The state can show
that sort of form of violence.

174
00:08:11,900 --> 00:08:13,966
What about abortion?
Do you agree with that?

175
00:08:14,026 --> 00:08:15,595
Yeah, but that's different, isn't it?

176
00:08:16,028 --> 00:08:19,832
I guess you can just arrange things
the way you like them.

177
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:25,404
-When you're rich, famous, like you.
-Yeah.

178
00:08:26,072 --> 00:08:27,139
Unlike you,

179
00:08:27,766 --> 00:08:31,000
who's just a guy with an old car

180
00:08:31,066 --> 00:08:34,266
going along the highway,
laughing maniacally

181
00:08:34,799 --> 00:08:37,533
'cause someone feels they're about to die.

182
00:08:38,666 --> 00:08:41,600
I should have said "let's do it"
when I got in this car.

183
00:08:49,929 --> 00:08:51,497
[steam hisses]

184
00:08:52,033 --> 00:08:53,333
[woman] You guys want coffee?

185
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:54,500
I would love some.

186
00:08:54,567 --> 00:08:57,203
-Just a regular filtered coffee with milk.
-I'll have half and half.

187
00:09:01,533 --> 00:09:04,199
Could I have two yellow eggs
and two egg whites?

188
00:09:05,211 --> 00:09:07,280
What do you mean by that?
You mean four eggs?

189
00:09:08,166 --> 00:09:11,133
-Two yolks and two whites.
-That's just two eggs.

190
00:09:11,183 --> 00:09:12,451
-That's four eggs.
-No.

191
00:09:12,518 --> 00:09:15,454
Two yolks and two whites is two eggs.
What do you mean?

192
00:09:16,589 --> 00:09:21,294
-Two additional whites on top of--
-Right, so four whites and two yolks.

193
00:09:21,727 --> 00:09:23,062
-That's correct.
-Yeah, yeah.

194
00:09:23,129 --> 00:09:26,632
You're like a young king, aren't you?
Things are kept from you.

195
00:09:26,999 --> 00:09:28,434
But you wanna do stuff.

196
00:09:28,501 --> 00:09:30,770
They go,
"He wants to drive around in a car."

197
00:09:31,270 --> 00:09:33,839
And someone said, "Well, just let him
drive around in the car."

198
00:09:33,906 --> 00:09:36,509
-"No, he wants to do it with celebrities."
-"He wants to do it

199
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:39,066
with some friends he's seen
on the telly."

200
00:09:39,199 --> 00:09:40,300
"Well, get their number."

201
00:09:40,766 --> 00:09:42,800
Would you like some jelly for your toast?

202
00:09:42,848 --> 00:09:44,850
[Ricky] No, I'm fine.
That's great, thank you.

203
00:09:51,266 --> 00:09:52,566
-How was your--
-How were your eggs?

204
00:09:57,663 --> 00:09:59,365
What are these guys doing here?

205
00:10:03,436 --> 00:10:04,370
It's Tuesday...

206
00:10:05,738 --> 00:10:06,839
at 11 o' clock.

207
00:10:07,300 --> 00:10:08,733
Who do you think you are?

208
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:11,066
You could be a good...

209
00:10:11,410 --> 00:10:15,081
lt's like a man strolling into town going,
"I want to find out what you're doing.

210
00:10:15,214 --> 00:10:16,415
"Just reading the paper."

211
00:10:16,633 --> 00:10:18,333
Make sure he's just read the paper.

212
00:10:19,366 --> 00:10:20,633
See you later.

213
00:10:23,699 --> 00:10:25,766
Although, the older I get...

214
00:10:26,600 --> 00:10:28,100
the more annoyed I am

215
00:10:28,166 --> 00:10:30,233
by what I call...

216
00:10:30,600 --> 00:10:31,966
"other people."

217
00:10:32,565 --> 00:10:35,701
There are people walking around. I'm
going, "Why are you scuffling your feet?

218
00:10:35,766 --> 00:10:37,100
There's no one to pick your feet up.

219
00:10:37,166 --> 00:10:40,100
Don't walk around with your jaw open.
Why do you eat chewing gum like that?

220
00:10:40,166 --> 00:10:42,400
Don't sniff that loudly in public.

221
00:10:42,566 --> 00:10:44,166
Don't spit on the floor."

222
00:10:44,233 --> 00:10:46,633
-Right, you might like Singapore.
-Why do you say that?

223
00:10:46,733 --> 00:10:48,800
-They take care of that stuff.
-Do they really?

224
00:10:48,848 --> 00:10:50,383
-Oh, yeah...
-There's etiquette police

225
00:10:50,449 --> 00:10:53,019
Yeah, you spit your gum on the street,
it's 50 lashes.

226
00:10:54,420 --> 00:10:56,155
Okay, that's going too far, isn't it?

227
00:10:56,222 --> 00:10:58,958
I don't think so.
Not if you really want it clean.

228
00:11:00,533 --> 00:11:02,966
Is that kosher?
You just had bacon.

229
00:11:05,699 --> 00:11:07,400
Slap the cuffs on, judge.

230
00:11:08,466 --> 00:11:10,566
-You got me.
-Listen, I don't make the rules.

231
00:11:10,800 --> 00:11:12,933
You want to, you said before,

232
00:11:12,972 --> 00:11:14,640
you want to be the mayor
that goes around and says,

233
00:11:14,707 --> 00:11:17,209
"Shut your mouth,
stop chewing your gum like that

234
00:11:17,276 --> 00:11:19,278
-and quit shuffling your feet."
-Yeah.

235
00:11:23,266 --> 00:11:27,800
What is the moment in history
that you would like to witness in person?

236
00:11:27,920 --> 00:11:30,122
I'll tell you mine just
to get the conversation rolling.

237
00:11:30,199 --> 00:11:32,266
-Okay.
-Gettysburg Address.

238
00:11:32,324 --> 00:11:35,227
I would like to have been in that crowd
and heard the Gettysburg Address.

239
00:11:35,961 --> 00:11:38,731
Churchill? Would you like to be there
when he's crafting a speech?

240
00:11:38,798 --> 00:11:42,501
How about being in Hitler's bunker
there as he's screaming?

241
00:11:42,568 --> 00:11:45,471
I'd be a bit nervous,
but then, after that car journey,

242
00:11:45,538 --> 00:11:48,374
Hitler doesn't scare me as much.
I'll be honest.

243
00:11:50,009 --> 00:11:51,343
[Ricky] Thank you.

244
00:11:51,410 --> 00:11:54,280
On the last day,
the Russians were advancing.

245
00:11:54,346 --> 00:11:57,483
They were 200 yards down the road.

246
00:11:57,550 --> 00:11:59,251
He married Eva Braun, right?

247
00:11:59,318 --> 00:12:02,521
They had ordered cake and some champagne.

248
00:12:02,888 --> 00:12:04,857
They retired to bed early.

249
00:12:04,966 --> 00:12:06,533
In the morning,

250
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:11,199
he poisoned her and shot himself.

251
00:12:11,333 --> 00:12:14,199
And the gardener burned the bodies, right?

252
00:12:14,266 --> 00:12:16,268
-Now, say what you will about Hitler...
-Yeah.

253
00:12:16,335 --> 00:12:19,271
But that's a terrible honeymoon, isn't it?
That's a crap honeymoon.

254
00:12:22,708 --> 00:12:25,945
The funniest part of that joke is,
"Say what you will about Hitler."

255
00:12:29,548 --> 00:12:32,184
I'm gonna go to the toilet
and take a Valium for the journey back.

256
00:12:32,251 --> 00:12:33,285
[laughing]

257
00:12:33,366 --> 00:12:34,866
If that's okay.

258
00:12:36,922 --> 00:12:38,891
[engine revving]

259
00:12:41,794 --> 00:12:44,263
[laughing]

260
00:12:44,330 --> 00:12:45,364
Right. Okay.

261
00:12:59,011 --> 00:13:00,579
[laughs]

262
00:13:01,547 --> 00:13:04,750
Jesus, this is like free falling.

263
00:13:04,817 --> 00:13:07,319
I know, it's fantastic.
Don't you feel alive?

264
00:13:07,386 --> 00:13:08,687
Yeah.

265
00:13:08,754 --> 00:13:10,923
-Even if only for a few more minutes.
-Shut up!

266
00:13:12,491 --> 00:13:15,928
[Jerry] I bet you this car looks great.
I'm in heaven.

267
00:13:17,166 --> 00:13:18,833
Do you think we look gay?

268
00:13:18,931 --> 00:13:20,332
The least of my worries.

269
00:13:20,399 --> 00:13:23,536
We're in stylish sunglasses,
we got the top down...

270
00:13:23,602 --> 00:13:25,304
-Yeah.
-in a poncy roadster.

271
00:13:25,371 --> 00:13:27,106
-Yeah.
-I think we look gay.

272
00:13:27,173 --> 00:13:30,309
I can just imagine you saying that
when our parachutes didn't open.

273
00:13:30,409 --> 00:13:32,945
And I'm all nervous.

274
00:13:33,279 --> 00:13:37,016
You think we look gay?
It doesn't matter... it doesn't matter.

275
00:13:37,082 --> 00:13:39,285
[laughing]

276
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:42,454
[Jerry continues laughing]

277
00:13:42,521 --> 00:13:44,256
[Ricky] I'm never gonna do this again.

278
00:13:44,390 --> 00:13:46,892
[Jerry cackles]

279
00:13:47,533 --> 00:13:48,800
[Ricky] Whoa, what the [bleep]

